[
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "# Secure Your UNS in 11 Rules — solution\n\nDeploy a worked access-control design for a small factory namespace: one reusable Model, one wildcard raw-to-clean Action, a probe Action, then eleven prioritized Rules. The Rules are the deliverable.\n\n**Operators:** README **Production readiness** (create role users, deploy Rules first in production, omit the simulator). Compose, `scripts/probe_access.py`, and the README are in this template folder on GitHub: [https://github.com/OWNER/REPO](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO) (path `templates/building-blocks/uns-security-starter/`).\n**Maintainers:** see `AUTHORING.md` for design decisions and how to extend this template.\n\n## Demo sequence (hardware-free)\n\n1. As `root`, create the three demo role users in Coreflux HUB: **System** → **User Management** (see README Quick start). MQTT `-addUser` is an alternative.\n2. Deploy this notebook in **HUB LoT Editor** or in the **LoT Notebooks extension** (VS Code or Cursor): connect as `root`, run cells top to bottom (Model → Actions → Rules last). MQTT `-addModel` / `-addAction` / `-addRule` is an alternative.\n3. Run `uns-security-starter-simulator.lotnb` the same way for allowed raw traffic.\n4. Verify in Coreflux HUB Data Viewer as each role (subscribe the catalog filters, not `#`). MQTT Explorer is an alternative. Optionally run `scripts/probe_access.py`.\n\n## Production sequence\n\nCreate production users in HUB **User Management**, deploy Rules **first** from HUB LoT Editor or this notebook, then the Model and Actions. Do not run the simulator. Full checklist: README Production readiness.\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\nraw/factory/<line>/<device>/temperature  →  wildcard Action + Model  →  factory/<line>/<device>/reading\nMQTT clients as role users  →  Rules (allow / deny)  →  factory/# and raw/factory/#\n```\n\n## Role catalog (create before Rules)\n\nCreate these users in HUB **System** → **User Management** before deploying Rules. Teaching size is three named users. Tenant isolation is a README adaptation, not extra notebook users.\n\nEvery client must connect **with a username and password**. The broker accepts anonymous connections by default, and an anonymous session is denied by every Rule here, which looks like an empty topic tree rather than an error. See **Troubleshooting** at the end of this notebook.\n\n| Username | Intended access |\n|---|---|\n| `dashboard` | Subscribe `factory/#` only. No publish anywhere. |\n| `device_x` | Publish and subscribe only `raw/factory/line1/device-x/#`. No clean-tree access. |\n| `engineer` | Publish and subscribe `factory/#` and `raw/factory/#` only. Subscribe those two filters, not `#`. |\n| `root` | Admin / deploying identity. All operations. |\n\nVerify in **Coreflux HUB** first (`http://localhost:8080` on compose): sign in as the role, open **MQTT** → **Data Viewer**, and subscribe the filters in the table, not `#`. HUB is an MQTT client, so Rules apply to that identity. MQTT Explorer is an alternative; those GUIs often default to `#`, which the catch-all denies for everyone except `root`.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "## Models\n\nDefine the reusable shape for every clean factory reading. This `COLLAPSED` Model has no fixed topic or trigger; the wildcard Action supplies all values and chooses the destination for each line and device.\n\n**Reads:** none\n**Writes:** none by itself; `BuildFactoryDeviceReading` publishes it\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE MODEL FactoryDeviceReading COLLAPSED\n    ADD STRING \"line_id\"\n    ADD STRING \"device_id\"\n    ADD DOUBLE \"temperature\"\n    ADD STRING \"unit\"\n    ADD STRING \"timestamp\""
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "## Actions\n\nBuild the clean UNS reading for every line and device with one wildcard Action. A message on `raw/factory/<line>/<device>/temperature` supplies the measurement, topic positions 3 and 4 supply the identifiers, and wildcard inheritance reads the matching live engineering-unit topic.\n\n**Reads:** `raw/factory/+/+/{temperature,temperature_EU}`\n**Writes:** `factory/<line>/<device>/reading` via `PUBLISH MODEL FactoryDeviceReading`\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE ACTION BuildFactoryDeviceReading\nON TOPIC \"raw/factory/+/+/temperature\" DO\n    SET \"line_id\" WITH TOPIC POSITION 3\n    SET \"device_id\" WITH TOPIC POSITION 4\n    SET \"temperature\" WITH PAYLOAD AS DOUBLE\n    SET \"unit\" WITH (GET TOPIC \"raw/factory/+/+/temperature_EU\" AS STRING)\n    PUBLISH MODEL FactoryDeviceReading TO \"factory/\" + {line_id} + \"/\" + {device_id} + \"/reading\" WITH\n        line_id = {line_id}\n        device_id = {device_id}\n        temperature = {temperature}\n        unit = {unit}\n        timestamp = TIMESTAMP \"UTC\""
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "Publish the expected allow/deny matrix as retained state, one topic per role, so you can compare the design against live client tests. `ON START` runs it once when the Action is deployed and again on every broker start, so the expectations are in the tree before you begin testing.\n\nOne topic per role on purpose: repeated `PUBLISH TOPIC` calls to a single topic would leave only the last line visible in an MQTT client. `KEEP TOPIC` retains each line so a client that connects later still receives it.\n\n**Reads:** none\n**Writes:** retained `factory/security/probe/{dashboard,device_x,engineer,source}`\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE ACTION PublishSecurityProbeGuide\nON START DO\n    KEEP TOPIC \"factory/security/probe/dashboard\" WITH \"ALLOW subscribe factory/# | DENY subscribe raw/factory/# | DENY publish anywhere\"\n    KEEP TOPIC \"factory/security/probe/device_x\" WITH \"ALLOW publish and subscribe raw/factory/line1/device-x/# | DENY the clean tree and other devices\"\n    KEEP TOPIC \"factory/security/probe/engineer\" WITH \"ALLOW publish and subscribe factory/# and raw/factory/# | DENY # and anything outside those two trees\"\n    KEEP TOPIC \"factory/security/probe/source\" WITH \"PublishSecurityProbeGuide ON START\""
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "## Routes\n\nThis template has no Routes. Topic security is enforced by Rules on the local broker. Skip this section in simulation.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "## Rules\n\nOfficial reference (docs.coreflux.org): [Rules overview](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/lot-language/rules/overview), [Rules syntax](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/lot-language/rules/syntax), [RBAC](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/lot-language/rules/rbac). Deploy by pasting `DEFINE RULE` into [HUB LoT Editor](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/coreflux-hub/lot) or by running these cells in the [LoT Notebooks extension](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/quick-start/vscode) (VS Code or Cursor). MQTT `-addRule` / `-removeRule` is an alternative ([broker commands](https://docs.coreflux.org/latest/mqtt-broker/commands)).\n\n**Notebook order puts Rules last on purpose:** a deny-by-default set applied mid-notebook can lock the deploying identity out of remaining cells. In production, deploy Rules **first** (see README Production readiness).\n\nSubscribe decisions are taken when a client subscribes: a subscription created **after** a Rule is deployed is evaluated against it (verified). Whether a subscription established **before** deployment is re-evaluated was not measured, so reconnect your test clients after deploying Rules instead of trusting a session that was already open.\n\n### Access map this set implements\n\nDeny by default, then grant each role its topics:\n\n- `dashboard`: subscribe `factory/#` only; every publish is denied.\n- `device_x`: publish and subscribe only `raw/factory/line1/device-x/#`.\n- `engineer`: publish and subscribe `factory/#` and `raw/factory/#` only.\n- `root`: all operations, including `$SYS` and user creation.\n\n**Connect as `engineer` and the tree is empty?** The grant is those two filters, not `#`. In Coreflux HUB Data Viewer, sign in as `engineer` and subscribe `factory/#` plus `raw/factory/#`. A `#` subscription is denied by the catch-all (by design). MQTT Explorer is an alternative and often defaults to `#`.\n\n### Two hard constraints this set is built around\n\n1. **Every rule must close with `ELSE`.** A rule written as `IF ... THEN DENY` with no `ELSE` leaves the block unclosed and does not compile, so the broker rejects it. Published doc examples still show `ELSE`-less rules; do not copy them.\n2. **A matching rule always decides.** Because every rule returns a verdict, the first rule whose scope and topic pattern match the operation produces the outcome. There is no \"deny this user and let everyone else fall through to a later rule\".\n\nTogether these force the ordering discipline below: **most specific overlapping topic gets the lowest priority number, deny-by-default catch-all gets the highest.** Publish and Subscribe are different operations, so a write rule and a read rule for the same tree share a number. A broad rule on the same operation placed too early would shadow every specific rule beneath it.\n\n### Both catch-alls are mandatory\n\nA Rule is matched against the topic filter a client literally requests, not against every topic that filter would overlap (verified). A client can therefore escape any specific Rule by asking one level broader: `raw/#` and `#` match none of the `raw/factory/...` or `factory/#` patterns and fall through to the broker's unconditional defaults at priority 1000000.\n\nSo the set needs a catch-all per direction: Rule 10 for publish and Rule 11 for subscribe. Ship both or the access map is advisory only.\n\n### A specific Rule shadows the broader Rules behind it\n\nThis is the consequence of constraint 2 that surprises people, so it is worth stating as its own rule of thumb: **a narrow Rule must re-list every identity the broader Rules grant.** Because the first matching Rule decides and nothing falls through, a user who is allowed by a broad Rule is still denied inside a narrow Rule that does not name them.\n\nThat is why Rules 4 and 7 name `engineer` and `root` alongside `device_x`, even though Rules 5 and 8 already grant engineer the whole `raw/factory/#` tree. Verified by removing `engineer` from Rule 4: engineer's publish to `raw/factory/line1/device-x/temperature` stopped landing, even with Rule 5 untouched. Restoring the name restored the access.\n\nSo when you add a device Rule, copy the broader grants into it. A device Rule that names only its own device silently revokes engineer's access to that subtree.\n\n### Editing a deployed Rule needs an explicit remove\n\nA Rule with the same name is **not overwritten**. Re-running an edited Rule cell is a silent no-op: the add fails with `A rule with the name '<name>' already exists`, and the old Rule keeps deciding. Verified on 2.14.3-rc.62; Models and Actions behave the opposite way (re-add replaces in place).\n\nRemove the Rule first in **HUB LoT Editor** (or delete it from the Coreflux sidebar in the LoT extension), then re-run the cell. **Alternative:** MQTT `-removeRule AllowDeviceXRawPublish` then `-addRule`. Watch `$SYS/Coreflux/Command/Output` for `\"success\":true` if you use commands, and read `$SYS/Coreflux/Rules/<name>` when in doubt.\n\n### Priority layers (publish and subscribe share a number)\n\nPriorities **0-99 are reserved for broker system Rules.** User Rules start at **100**, which is the highest user-controlled priority available to this template because lower numbers evaluate first. Priorities do **not** have to be unique: docs examples put several different-scope rules at the same number. This set uses one number per topic layer and splits Publish from Subscribe:\n\n| Layer | Priority | Publish | Subscribe |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| User creation | 100 | `RestrictUserManagement` (`UserManagementCreation`) | — |\n| `$SYS` | 110 | `ProtectSysPublish` | `ProtectSysSubscribe` |\n| Device-x raw (most specific) | 120 | `AllowDeviceXRawPublish` | `AllowDeviceXRawSubscribe` |\n| Engineer raw (broader) | 130 | `AllowEngineerRawPublish` | `AllowEngineerRawSubscribe` |\n| Clean factory | 140 | `RestrictFactoryPublish` | `AllowFactorySubscribe` |\n| Deny by default | 200 | `DenyDefaultPublish` | `DenyDefaultSubscribe` |\n\nSame-operation overlapping patterns still need different numbers (120 before 130) because a publish to `raw/factory/line1/device-x/temperature` matches both the device-x pattern and `raw/factory/#`. Same-operation same-priority order is not documented, so this set does not rely on it.\n\nIf an earlier copy of this notebook is already deployed, remove the four subscribe rules whose priority changed in HUB LoT Editor or the LoT extension sidebar, then re-run their cells: `AllowDeviceXRawSubscribe`, `AllowEngineerRawSubscribe`, `AllowFactorySubscribe`, `DenyDefaultSubscribe`. MQTT `-removeRule` on those names is an alternative.\n\n**Relationship to broker defaults (verified on 2.14.3-rc.62):** the broker ships named default Rules, readable under `$SYS/Coreflux/Rules/`, and they do not currently sit inside the reserved 0-99 band:\n\n| Default Rule | Priority | Effect on this set |\n|---|---|---|\n| `AllowUserCreation` | 10 | Matches `UserManagementCreation` before Rule 1, so Rule 1 does not decide today |\n| `AllowSubscribeSysClient` | 10 | Matches `$SYS/#` subscribe before Rule 3, so Rule 3 does not decide today |\n| `AllowPublishSysCommand` | 10 | Matches the command topic before Rule 2 |\n| `AllowPublishTopic` | 1000000 | Open publish catch-all; every Rule below wins over it |\n| `AllowSubscribeTopic` | 1000000 | Open subscribe catch-all; every Rule below wins over it |\n\nSo Rules 4-11 are the live policy on this broker, while Rules 1-3 are currently pre-empted by defaults at priority 10 that already restrict the same operations to privileged users. Keep Rules 1-3: they are the correct expression of the intent, they collide with nothing, and they become the deciding Rules once defaults move into the reserved band. Read `$SYS/Coreflux/Rules/` on your own broker before assuming a Rule at 100+ decides a management or `$SYS` operation.\n\n**Model and Action publishes (verified on 2.14.3-rc.62):** the two publish forms behave differently, and this is the single most important thing to know before writing a Rule over a tree your own entities write to.\n\n| Entity operation | Subject to `Publish` Rules? | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PUBLISH TOPIC` inside an Action | **No** | Rule 5 restricts `raw/factory/#` to `engineer`/`root`, yet the simulator keeps publishing raw at full cadence. A throwaway root-only Rule on one device subtree did not interrupt it either. |\n| `PUBLISH MODEL` inside an Action | **Yes** | An allow-list on `factory/#` naming `engineer`/`root` silenced the clean readings completely. Removing it restored them within one cycle. |\n\nThe identity behind `PUBLISH MODEL` matches no `USER IS` condition and holds no session in `$SYS/Coreflux/Comms/Clients`, so it cannot be named in an allow-list. It is indistinguishable from an unauthenticated client. That is why Rule 6 is a deny-list while every other topic Rule is an allow-list, and why disabling anonymous login is a hard requirement rather than a nicety.\n\n### Every client must authenticate\n\nThis broker ships `AnonymousLogin: true` (see `$SYS/Coreflux/Config`), so a client with no username connects successfully and is then treated as **no user**. Ten of the eleven Rules close with `ELSE DENY`, so an anonymous session matches the `ELSE` branch and is denied. The exception is Rule 6, which must allow the unnameable identity behind `PUBLISH MODEL` and therefore allows anonymous publish on `factory/#` too.\n\nSo anonymous clients are denied almost everywhere by construction, and the one hole is closed at the broker rather than in LOT: **disable anonymous login.** A denied anonymous session is also the most likely reason a tree looks empty after deploying these Rules, because the client is anonymous rather than `root`. Verified behavior with Rule 9 active: `root` and `dashboard` receive `Granted QoS 0` and messages flow, while an anonymous session receives SUBACK `0x80` and zero messages. Set a username and password on every client, including Coreflux HUB (and MQTT Explorer if you use it), and disable anonymous login before production (README **Production readiness**).\n\n### Rule 1 - Restrict user creation (priority 100)\n\nUser story: **only `root` creates broker users.**\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE RestrictUserManagement WITH PRIORITY 100 FOR UserManagementCreation\n    IF USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 2 - Protect `$SYS` publish (priority 110)\n\nOnly `root` may publish to system topics (command channel and related `$SYS` traffic).\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE ProtectSysPublish WITH PRIORITY 110 FOR PublishSys TO TOPIC \"$SYS/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 3 - Protect `$SYS` subscribe (priority 110)\n\nOnly `root` may subscribe to `$SYS/#` (broker internals and command output).\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE ProtectSysSubscribe WITH PRIORITY 110 FOR SubscribeSys TO TOPIC \"$SYS/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 4 - Device-x raw write (priority 120)\n\nUser story: **device_x publishes only under `raw/factory/line1/device-x/#`.** Engineer and root are also allowed here so OT can inject or correct ingress.\n\nSubscribe twin is Rule 7 at the same priority: Publish and Subscribe never compete. This is the **most specific** raw publish rule. Its `ELSE DENY` stops `dashboard` (and any other identity) from writing into this device's ingress.\n\n**Naming `engineer` and `root` here is mandatory, not generosity.** This Rule shadows Rule 5 for every topic under `raw/factory/line1/device-x/`, so engineer's `raw/factory/#` grant does not reach inside it. Verified: dropping `engineer` from this condition stopped engineer's publishes to `raw/factory/line1/device-x/temperature` from landing while Rule 5 was untouched.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE AllowDeviceXRawPublish WITH PRIORITY 120 FOR Publish TO TOPIC \"raw/factory/line1/device-x/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"device_x\" OR USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 5 - Engineer raw write (priority 130)\n\nUser story: **engineer may publish anywhere under `raw/factory/#`.** Broader than rule 4, so `device_x` writing `raw/factory/line1/device-y/...` lands here and is denied by `ELSE`. Subscribe twin is Rule 8 at the same priority.\n\nRead \"anywhere\" with one caveat: this grant does not reach inside any narrower Rule. Topics under `raw/factory/line1/device-x/` are decided by Rule 4, which is why engineer is named there too.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE AllowEngineerRawPublish WITH PRIORITY 130 FOR Publish TO TOPIC \"raw/factory/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 6 - Clean-tree write (priority 140)\n\nUser story: **engineer may publish the clean factory UNS; dashboard and device_x may not.** Subscribe twin is Rule 9 at the same priority.\n\n**This is the one Rule in the set written as a deny-list, and it must stay that way.** The clean tree is written by `BuildFactoryDeviceReading` via `PUBLISH MODEL`, and `PUBLISH MODEL` **is** subject to `Publish` Rules under an identity that matches no `USER IS` condition. An allow-list here (`IF USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN ALLOW ELSE DENY`) therefore denies the Action's own output and the clean tree goes silent, which is exactly the failure this set shipped with. Verified on 2.14.3-rc.62: removing that allow-list Rule restored the readings immediately, and this deny-list keeps them flowing while still denying `dashboard` and `device_x`.\n\nNote the contrast with Rules 4 and 5: `PUBLISH TOPIC` from an Action is **not** rule-governed, so the raw tree can keep its allow-list while this Rule cannot.\n\n**Consequence you must accept:** `ELSE ALLOW` also allows an unauthenticated client to publish here, because the broker cannot distinguish the Model's identity from an anonymous one. **Disable anonymous login** on any broker where this matters (README **Production readiness**). Migrating an earlier copy: `-removeRule AllowEngineerFactoryPublish`, since this Rule is renamed.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE RestrictFactoryPublish WITH PRIORITY 140 FOR Publish TO TOPIC \"factory/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"dashboard\" OR USER IS \"device_x\" THEN\n        DENY\n    ELSE\n        ALLOW"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 7 - Device-x raw read (priority 120)\n\nUser story: **device_x may subscribe only its own raw subtree** (config or echo). Engineer and root can read it too.\n\nSame priority as Rule 4: Subscribe does not compete with Publish. The number 120 still beats Rule 8 at 130, so this Rule decides inside the device-x subtree.\n\nAs in Rule 4, naming `engineer` and `root` is required: this Rule shadows Rule 8 inside the device-x subtree, so omitting them would revoke engineer's read access to exactly the subtree Rule 8 appears to grant.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE AllowDeviceXRawSubscribe WITH PRIORITY 120 FOR Subscribe TO TOPIC \"raw/factory/line1/device-x/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"device_x\" OR USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 8 - Engineer raw read (priority 130)\n\nUser story: **engineer may subscribe all factory ingress.** Dashboard matching `raw/factory/#` is denied by `ELSE`, so the dashboard never sees raw topics.\n\nSame priority as Rule 5. Same caveat as Rule 5: filters inside `raw/factory/line1/device-x/` are decided by Rule 7, not here.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE AllowEngineerRawSubscribe WITH PRIORITY 130 FOR Subscribe TO TOPIC \"raw/factory/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 9 - Dashboard (and engineer) clean-tree read (priority 140)\n\nUser story: **dashboard is read-only on the clean UNS.** Engineer is named here so OT can subscribe `factory/#` in Coreflux HUB Data Viewer (`#` will not do; MQTT Explorer is an alternative with the same filter). `device_x` is deliberately absent: the device does not consume `factory/#`. Any other identity is denied.\n\nSame priority as Rule 6.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE AllowFactorySubscribe WITH PRIORITY 140 FOR Subscribe TO TOPIC \"factory/+\"\n    IF USER IS \"dashboard\" OR USER IS \"engineer\" OR USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 10 - Publish deny-by-default (priority 200)\n\nCatch-all for every remaining standard-topic publish (`#`). Dashboard cannot publish anywhere. Engineer cannot publish outside `factory/#` and `raw/factory/#` (those matched earlier). Only `root` is allowed here.\n\nSame priority as Rule 11: Publish does not compete with Subscribe. Highest layer on purpose, so it never shadows the specific publish rules above it. `$SYS` uses `PublishSys`, not this rule.\n\nIf Model or simulator publishes stop after this rule deploys, see `VERIFICATION.md` (entity publish identity).\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE DenyDefaultPublish WITH PRIORITY 200 FOR Publish TO TOPIC \"#\"\n    IF USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "### Rule 11 - Subscribe deny-by-default (priority 200)\n\n**Without this Rule the entire read side of the access map is bypassable.** Subscribe Rules are matched against the filter the client literally asks for, not against every topic the filter would overlap. So `raw/factory/#` matches Rule 8 and is denied for `device_x`, but `raw/#` and `#` match **none** of the specific patterns above and fall through to the broker default `AllowSubscribeTopic` (`#`, priority 1000000, unconditional `ALLOW`).\n\nVerified on 2.14.3-rc.62 before this Rule existed: `device_x` subscribing `raw/#` was granted and received device-y telemetry, and subscribing `#` additionally received the whole clean tree. `dashboard` leaked identically on `#`. After adding this Rule, both are denied on `raw/#` and `#` while keeping their intended access.\n\nThis is the read-side twin of Rule 10, at the same priority 200. It never shadows the specific subscribe allows at 120-140, which have lower numbers.\n\nIf admin tooling connects as something other than `root`, widen the condition with `OR USER HAS AllowedSystemConfiguration` rather than deleting the Rule.\n"
  },
  {
    "kind": 2,
    "language": "lot",
    "value": "DEFINE RULE DenyDefaultSubscribe WITH PRIORITY 200 FOR Subscribe TO TOPIC \"#\"\n    IF USER IS \"root\" THEN\n        ALLOW\n    ELSE\n        DENY"
  },
  {
    "kind": 1,
    "language": "markdown",
    "value": "## Negative tests (observe DENY live)\n\nRules evaluate the **MQTT client username**, not simulator Actions. Watch ALLOW and DENY in Coreflux HUB Data Viewer signed in as each role. Subscribe the catalog filters, not `#`. MQTT Explorer is an alternative.\n\n| User | Subscribe ALLOW | Subscribe DENY | Publish ALLOW | Publish DENY |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `dashboard` | `factory/#` | `raw/factory/#`, `#` | none | anywhere |\n| `device_x` | `raw/factory/line1/device-x/#` | `raw/#`, `#`, `factory/#` | own raw subtree | other devices, clean tree |\n| `engineer` | `factory/#`, `raw/factory/#` | `#` | those two trees | anything else |\n\nAlways test one level broader than the Rule you wrote. `raw/factory/#` being denied says nothing about `raw/#`.\n\nOptional listen check (`python3 -m pip install paho-mqtt` once). From this template folder, use the passwords you set in HUB User Management:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/probe_access.py --dashboard-pass '...' --device-x-pass '...' --engineer-pass '...'\n```\n\nEach line is yes or no. Confirm writes in HUB Data Viewer.\n\n## Troubleshooting: the tree looks empty\n\nIf a tree looks empty after deploying these Rules, check two things: **who signed in**, and **which filter they subscribed**. Verify in **Coreflux HUB** first (`http://localhost:8080`): sign in as the role, open **MQTT** → **Data Viewer**, and subscribe the catalog filters, not `#`.\n\n**HUB as `engineer` (or `dashboard`) shows nothing.** The session subscribed `#`, and Rule 11 denies `#` for everyone except `root`. That is deny-by-default working, not a missing grant. Subscribe the filters from the role catalog: `engineer` uses `factory/#` and `raw/factory/#`; `dashboard` uses `factory/#` only; `device_x` uses `raw/factory/line1/device-x/#`.\n\n**Alternative: MQTT Explorer** (or any other MQTT GUI). Same username, password, and filters. Those clients often default to `#`.\n\n**Anonymous client.** The broker allows anonymous connections, so a client with no credentials connects normally and is then denied by every `ELSE DENY` branch. A denied subscription returns SUBACK `0x80`; many MQTT clients show this as a silent empty tree rather than an error.\n\n**If the clean tree is silent while `raw/factory/#` still flows,** the cause is a `Publish` Rule over `factory/#` written as an allow-list. `PUBLISH MODEL` is rule-governed under an unnameable identity, so an allow-list denies the Action's own output. Rule 6 ships as a deny-list for exactly this reason; if you convert it to an allow-list, the readings stop. Confirm by removing that Rule in HUB LoT Editor (or the LoT extension) and watching the readings return within one cycle. MQTT `-removeRule` is an alternative.\n\nRaw ingress from the simulator is never the cause: `PUBLISH TOPIC` inside an Action is not rule-governed.\n\nThe probe Action publishes the expected matrix as retained topics under `factory/security/probe/` when it is deployed and on every broker start, so an authenticated client sees the intended design next to the live results.\n"
  }
]
